1. CMS redistributes outpatient margin away from 340B drugs and legacy off-ca=
mpus sites
What happened
CMS finalized a 2.3% OPPS and ASC rate update for 2027, but will pay for 340B=
-acquired drugs at average sales price minus 28%. CMS estimates the drug poli=
cy will reduce Original Medicare payments by $4.35 billion and beneficiary pa=
yments by $1.1 billion, with Medicare savings redistributed to non-drug OPPS =
services through budget neutrality. Hospitals enrolled before 2018 also face =
a separate 2% conversion-factor reduction through 2031 to recover prior 340B =
remedy payments. CMS additionally applied physician-office-equivalent rates t=
o noncontrast imaging at excepted off-campus departments and removed 637 proc=
edures from the inpatient-only list. (
CMS,
CY 2027 OPPS and ASC Final Rule
, November 4, 2026
)
Implication
The 2.3% headline conceals a large service-line redistribution. Systems gener=
ating disproportionate margin from 340B-infused drugs or legacy off-campus im=
aging will lose relative to procedure-heavy hospitals and ASCs receiving high=
er non-drug payments and broader surgical eligibility. Contracting, site-of-c=
are management and service-line profitability tools become more valuable as p=
roviders reassess infusion, imaging and surgical footprints.
Watch next
Hospital legal challenges, 2027 drug-administration strategies and whether pa=
yers accelerate migration of newly eligible procedures to ASCs.
2. Physician fee cuts coexist with richer ACO payment and a two-year remote-m=
onitoring reprieve
What happened
CMS finalized 2027 conversion factors of $32.95 for qualifying APM participan=
ts and $32.63 for other clinicians, decreases of 1.83% and 2.31%, respectivel=
y. The rule converts G2211 into a modifier that increases associated E/M paym=
ent by 16% and creates a separate 32% modifier for practitioners participatin=
g in the Medicare Shared Savings Program or the LEAD Model. CMS also delayed =
until January 1, 2029, its requirement that remote physiologic and therapeuti=
c monitoring services use clinical staff employed by the billing practice rat=
her than contractors. (
CMS,
CY 2027 Medicare Physician Fee Schedule Final Rule
, November 4, 2026
)
Implication
Independent and employed physician groups face renewed base-rate pressure, st=
rengthening demand for coding accuracy, denial prevention and productivity au=
tomation. The ACO modifier creates a material differential for primary-care o=
rganizations operating inside accountable-care structures, although the addit=
ional payments enter assignment, benchmark and performance-year spending calc=
ulations.
The contractor delay preserves current RPM and RTM vendor models through 2028=
. Practices avoid an immediate insourcing requirement, and vendors gain two y=
ears to redesign staffing, contracting or software-only offerings.
Watch next
Specialty-level payment impacts, congressional action on the conversion-facto=
r decline and whether CMS revisits remote-monitoring bundling before 2029.
3. CVS preserves payer-PBM-care integration while separating the consumer fro=
nt door
What happened
CVS plans to create two publicly traded companies through a tax-free separati=
on expected in the first half of 2028. CVS Health will retain Aetna, Caremark=
, CVS Specialty, Oak Street Health and Signify Health. CVS Pharmacy will hold=
 approximately 9,000 retail pharmacies, mail pharmacy, MinuteClinic, virtual =
care and the Health100 technology business. Both companies are expected to ma=
intain investment-grade ratings. (
CVS Health,
CVS Health Announces Intent to Create Two Distinct Leaders in Healthcare
, November 4, 2026
)
Implication
The transaction narrows CVS=E2=80=99s vertical-integration thesis rather than=
 dismantling it. Aetna will remain connected to Caremark, specialty pharmacy,=
 primary care and home assessments, preserving the assets most directly tied =
to medical-cost management and pharmacy economics.
Retail pharmacy and MinuteClinic become a payer-agnostic contracting platform=
 with greater incentive to serve competing plans and PBMs. The split also cre=
ates new data, identity, referral and pharmacy-network interfaces where inter=
nal coordination previously sufficed. Vendors serving member engagement, inte=
roperability and care navigation may gain opportunity, while CVS must prevent=
 separation-related friction from degrading medication adherence or Aetna car=
e coordination.
Watch next
The Form 10 allocation of shared technology, data rights, debt and corporate =
costs, particularly ownership and commercial terms for Health100.
4. CMS gives home health a 2.4% increase while widening retroactive payment r=
ecovery
What happened
CMS estimates aggregate Medicare home-health payments will increase 2.4%, or =
$415 million, in 2027, while retaining a temporary 3% reduction to the base r=
ate under its PDGM reconciliation. The rule also makes Medicare enrollment re=
vocations retroactive to the date noncompliance began across provider and sup=
plier types, expands grounds for denial or revocation, and requires reenrollm=
ent after certain ownership changes involving home-health, hospice and DMEPOS=
 providers. CMS estimates the integrity provisions will save approximately $8=
2 million annually. (
CMS,
CY 2027 Home Health Prospective Payment System Final Rule
, November 4, 2026
;
CMS,
CMS Finalizes Broad Medicare Integrity Changes in Home Health Final Rule
, November 4, 2026
)
Implication
The payment update improves near-term reimbursement visibility for scaled hom=
e-health operators, but it does not end PDGM recoupment risk. Case-mix recali=
bration and the temporary adjustment will continue to create agency-level var=
iance.
Retroactive revocation has broader consequences. A compliance failure can now=
 convert previously paid claims into recoverable overpayments, increasing exp=
osure during audits, acquisitions and ownership transitions. Enrollment monit=
oring, credentialing and transaction diligence therefore become payment-integ=
rity controls rather than administrative formalities.
Watch next
Sub-regulatory instructions defining recovery periods and how Medicare contra=
ctors apply the new ownership and managing-employee standards.
5. Oscar shows Exchange underwriting can improve while hospitals absorb cover=
age loss
What happened
Oscar=E2=80=99s third-quarter revenue increased to $5.14 billion from $2.99 b=
illion, its MLR declined to 84.0% from 88.5%, and operating income improved t=
o $178 million from a $129 million loss. Membership reached 2.69 million, and=
 updated risk-adjustment estimates contributed $250 million to revenue. Oscar=
 raised full-year operating-income guidance to $850 million-$950 million from=
 $600 million-$800 million. (
Oscar Health,
Third Quarter 2026 Results
, November 5, 2026
)
Implication
The result does not refute last week=E2=80=99s finding that Exchange attritio=
n is increasing hospital uncompensated care. It localizes the economics: disc=
iplined rate increases and favorable risk adjustment can improve carrier prof=
itability on retained enrollment while providers bear losses when other consu=
mers exit coverage.
The split raises the value of enrollment retention, premium-payment monitorin=
g and eligibility intervention for both sides. Plans need accurate risk-adjus=
tment and lapse management; hospitals need earlier identification of coverage=
 loss before services convert to self-pay. Oscar=E2=80=99s $250 million adjus=
tment also shows that risk-score performance remains a major earnings variabl=
e despite improved underlying underwriting.
Watch next
Final 2027 enrollment, payment-effectuation rates and whether Oscar=E2=80=99s=
 risk-adjustment advantage persists after market-wide data reconcile.
