17px;color:#12395b;margin:22px 0 8px;">Executive Summary</h3>
<ul style=3D"margin:0 0 12px;padding-left:22px;">
<li style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-hei=
ght:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 6px;">CMS shifted 2027 outpatient economics =
away from 340B drug margin and legacy off-campus departments while expanding =
outpatient surgical eligibility, creating substantial redistribution across h=
ospital service lines beginning January 1. (<a href=3D"https://www.cms.gov/ne=
wsroom/fact-sheets/calendar-year-2027-hospital-outpatient-prospective-payment=
-system-opps-ambulatory-surgical-center-0" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decora=
tion:underline;">CMS, <em>CY 2027 OPPS and ASC Final Rule</em>, November 4, 2=
026</a>)</li>
<li style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-hei=
ght:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 6px;">Physician conversion factors will decl=
ine 1.83%-2.31%, but ACO participants gain a 32% E/M modifier and remote-moni=
toring companies retain contracted clinical-staff models through 2028. (<a hr=
ef=3D"https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/fact-sheets/calendar-year-cy-2027-medicare=
-physician-fee-schedule-final-rule" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:un=
derline;">CMS, <em>CY 2027 Medicare Physician Fee Schedule Final Rule</em>, N=
ovember 4, 2026</a>)</li>
<li style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-hei=
ght:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 6px;">CVS will separate retail pharmacy, Min=
uteClinic and Health100 from Aetna, Caremark, specialty pharmacy and value-ba=
sed care, preserving the core payer-PBM-care platform while detaching its con=
sumer storefront. (<a href=3D"https://investors.cvshealth.com/news/news-detai=
ls/2026/CVS-Health-Announces-Intent-to-Create-Two-Distinct-Leaders-in-Healthc=
are/default.aspx" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">CVS Heal=
th, <em>CVS Health Announces Intent to Create Two Distinct Leaders in Healthc=
are</em>, November 4, 2026</a>)</li>
<li style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-hei=
ght:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 6px;">Payer execution improved: Oscar lowere=
d its third-quarter MLR to 84.0%, while Humana reported a 93.7% benefit ratio=
 and medical and pharmacy trends in line to better than its high-single-digit=
 expectation. (<a href=3D"https://ir.oscarhealth.com/sec-filings/all-sec-fili=
ngs/content/0001568651-26-000082/oscarhealththirdquarter202.htm" style=3D"col=
or:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">Oscar Health, <em>Third Quarter 2026 R=
esults</em>, November 5, 2026</a>; <a href=3D"https://humana.gcs-web.com/stat=
ic-files/87a928cc-8abf-4636-9e85-370745b2ac58" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-de=
coration:underline;">Humana, <em>Third Quarter 2026 Prepared Management Remar=
ks</em>, November 6, 2026</a>)</li>
<li style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-hei=
ght:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 6px;">Overall environment: improved for paye=
rs and ACO-oriented primary care, deteriorated for physician fee-for-service =
and 340B-heavy providers, and mixed for health technology because remote-moni=
toring demand gained time while CVS=E2=80=99s separation creates new integrat=
ion and contracting risk.</li>
</ul>
<h3 style=3D"font-family:Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;font-size:17px;col=
or:#12395b;margin:22px 0 8px;"><a id=3D"story-1" name=3D"story-1" style=3D"co=
lor:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;"></a>1. CMS redistributes outpatient m=
argin away from 340B drugs and legacy off-campus sites</h3>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>What happened</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">CMS finalized a 2.3% OPPS and ASC rate=
 update for 2027, but will pay for 340B-acquired drugs at average sales price=
 minus 28%. CMS estimates the drug policy will reduce Original Medicare payme=
nts by $4.35 billion and beneficiary payments by $1.1 billion, with Medicare =
savings redistributed to non-drug OPPS services through budget neutrality. Ho=
spitals enrolled before 2018 also face a separate 2% conversion-factor reduct=
ion through 2031 to recover prior 340B remedy payments. CMS additionally appl=
ied physician-office-equivalent rates to noncontrast imaging at excepted off-=
campus departments and removed 637 procedures from the inpatient-only list. (=
<a href=3D"https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/fact-sheets/calendar-year-2027-hospit=
al-outpatient-prospective-payment-system-opps-ambulatory-surgical-center-0" s=
tyle=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">CMS, <em>CY 2027 OPPS and A=
SC Final Rule</em>, November 4, 2026</a>)</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Implication</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">The 2.3% headline conceals a large ser=
vice-line redistribution. Systems generating disproportionate margin from 340=
B-infused drugs or legacy off-campus imaging will lose relative to procedure-=
heavy hospitals and ASCs receiving higher non-drug payments and broader surgi=
cal eligibility. Contracting, site-of-care management and service-line profit=
ability tools become more valuable as providers reassess infusion, imaging an=
d surgical footprints.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Watch next</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Hospital legal challenges, 2027 drug-a=
dministration strategies and whether payers accelerate migration of newly eli=
gible procedures to ASCs.</p>
<hr style=3D"border:0;border-top:1px solid #d5dde3;margin:18px 0;"/>
<h3 style=3D"font-family:Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;font-size:17px;col=
or:#12395b;margin:22px 0 8px;"><a id=3D"story-2" name=3D"story-2" style=3D"co=
lor:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;"></a>2. Physician fee cuts coexist wit=
h richer ACO payment and a two-year remote-monitoring reprieve</h3>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>What happened</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">CMS finalized 2027 conversion factors =
of $32.95 for qualifying APM participants and $32.63 for other clinicians, de=
creases of 1.83% and 2.31%, respectively. The rule converts G2211 into a modi=
fier that increases associated E/M payment by 16% and creates a separate 32% =
modifier for practitioners participating in the Medicare Shared Savings Progr=
am or the LEAD Model. CMS also delayed until January 1, 2029, its requirement=
 that remote physiologic and therapeutic monitoring services use clinical sta=
ff employed by the billing practice rather than contractors. (<a href=3D"http=
s://www.cms.gov/newsroom/fact-sheets/calendar-year-cy-2027-medicare-physician=
-fee-schedule-final-rule" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">=
CMS, <em>CY 2027 Medicare Physician Fee Schedule Final Rule</em>, November 4,=
 2026</a>)</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Implication</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Independent and employed physician gro=
ups face renewed base-rate pressure, strengthening demand for coding accuracy=
, denial prevention and productivity automation. The ACO modifier creates a m=
aterial differential for primary-care organizations operating inside accounta=
ble-care structures, although the additional payments enter assignment, bench=
mark and performance-year spending calculations.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">The contractor delay preserves current=
 RPM and RTM vendor models through 2028. Practices avoid an immediate insourc=
ing requirement, and vendors gain two years to redesign staffing, contracting=
 or software-only offerings.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Watch next</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Specialty-level payment impacts, congr=
essional action on the conversion-factor decline and whether CMS revisits rem=
ote-monitoring bundling before 2029.</p>
<hr style=3D"border:0;border-top:1px solid #d5dde3;margin:18px 0;"/>
<h3 style=3D"font-family:Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;font-size:17px;col=
or:#12395b;margin:22px 0 8px;"><a id=3D"story-3" name=3D"story-3" style=3D"co=
lor:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;"></a>3. CVS preserves payer-PBM-care i=
ntegration while separating the consumer front door</h3>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>What happened</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">CVS plans to create two publicly trade=
d companies through a tax-free separation expected in the first half of 2028.=
 CVS Health will retain Aetna, Caremark, CVS Specialty, Oak Street Health and=
 Signify Health. CVS Pharmacy will hold approximately 9,000 retail pharmacies=
, mail pharmacy, MinuteClinic, virtual care and the Health100 technology busi=
ness. Both companies are expected to maintain investment-grade ratings. (<a h=
ref=3D"https://investors.cvshealth.com/news/news-details/2026/CVS-Health-Anno=
unces-Intent-to-Create-Two-Distinct-Leaders-in-Healthcare/default.aspx" style=
=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">CVS Health, <em>CVS Health Anno=
unces Intent to Create Two Distinct Leaders in Healthcare</em>, November 4, 2=
026</a>)</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Implication</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">The transaction narrows CVS=E2=80=99s =
vertical-integration thesis rather than dismantling it. Aetna will remain con=
nected to Caremark, specialty pharmacy, primary care and home assessments, pr=
eserving the assets most directly tied to medical-cost management and pharmac=
y economics.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Retail pharmacy and MinuteClinic becom=
e a payer-agnostic contracting platform with greater incentive to serve compe=
ting plans and PBMs. The split also creates new data, identity, referral and =
pharmacy-network interfaces where internal coordination previously sufficed. =
Vendors serving member engagement, interoperability and care navigation may g=
ain opportunity, while CVS must prevent separation-related friction from degr=
ading medication adherence or Aetna care coordination.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Watch next</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">The Form 10 allocation of shared techn=
ology, data rights, debt and corporate costs, particularly ownership and comm=
ercial terms for Health100.</p>
