<h3 style=3D"font-family:Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;font-size:17px;col=
or:#12395b;margin:22px 0 8px;"><a id=3D"story-4" name=3D"story-4" style=3D"co=
lor:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;"></a>4. CMS gives home health a 2.4% i=
ncrease while widening retroactive payment recovery</h3>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>What happened</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">CMS estimates aggregate Medicare home-=
health payments will increase 2.4%, or $415 million, in 2027, while retaining=
 a temporary 3% reduction to the base rate under its PDGM reconciliation. The=
 rule also makes Medicare enrollment revocations retroactive to the date nonc=
ompliance began across provider and supplier types, expands grounds for denia=
l or revocation, and requires reenrollment after certain ownership changes in=
volving home-health, hospice and DMEPOS providers. CMS estimates the integrit=
y provisions will save approximately $82 million annually. (<a href=3D"https:=
//www.cms.gov/newsroom/fact-sheets/calendar-year-cy-2027-home-health-prospect=
ive-payment-system-final-rule-cms-1844-f" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decorat=
ion:underline;">CMS, <em>CY 2027 Home Health Prospective Payment System Final=
 Rule</em>, November 4, 2026</a>; <a href=3D"https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/pre=
ss-releases/cms-finalizes-broad-medicare-integrity-changes-home-health-final-=
rule-expected-save-taxpayers-tens" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:und=
erline;">CMS, <em>CMS Finalizes Broad Medicare Integrity Changes in Home Heal=
th Final Rule</em>, November 4, 2026</a>)</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Implication</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">The payment update improves near-term =
reimbursement visibility for scaled home-health operators, but it does not en=
d PDGM recoupment risk. Case-mix recalibration and the temporary adjustment w=
ill continue to create agency-level variance.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Retroactive revocation has broader con=
sequences. A compliance failure can now convert previously paid claims into r=
ecoverable overpayments, increasing exposure during audits, acquisitions and =
ownership transitions. Enrollment monitoring, credentialing and transaction d=
iligence therefore become payment-integrity controls rather than administrati=
ve formalities.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Watch next</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Sub-regulatory instructions defining r=
ecovery periods and how Medicare contractors apply the new ownership and mana=
ging-employee standards.</p>
<hr style=3D"border:0;border-top:1px solid #d5dde3;margin:18px 0;"/>
<h3 style=3D"font-family:Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;font-size:17px;col=
or:#12395b;margin:22px 0 8px;"><a id=3D"story-5" name=3D"story-5" style=3D"co=
